<p>Conversation with SCP-XXXX instances is limited due to them preferring to talk with Hennessy</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Awkward phrasing. Try &quot;Conversation with SCP-XXXX instances is limited as they prefer to talk only with Hennessy.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They've even told me what they're plan is for me.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;They're&quot; should be &quot;their&quot;</p>
<p>You refer to Hennessy by object pronouns throughout this. I'm mentioning it here as I'm not sure if that's an error or not, as it's possible that this character goes by it/it's, however, it reads as though this might have been an error to me.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Many of the structural issues from the previous version remain here. Notably, the ending is still fairly weak. Although it's clearer what it literally means now, it's still poor payoff because it's not well established and setup.</p>
<p>This is something I touched on last time, but Hennessy's &quot;problems&quot; are far too vaguely defined. As an article built on the basis of &quot;this man needs to improve himself, but the efforts of others to do so end up being harmful&quot;, it is essential that the reader clearly understands what those stakes are and what is being improved upon, but that's really not clearly set up enough.</p>
<p>Additionally to this, it's not clear what you and what the article thinks about these problems; the subtext on the lessons and the SCP-XXXX-X instances is clear, they're bad, but the problems themselves that are being worked on have no clear moral alignment within the article's subtext. Leaving it ambiguous or grey would be fine if not for the fact that the audience needs that judgement to be able to fully engage with the article's commentary on the -X instances. The problems are referenced vaguely, particularly drinking, but because the lessons don't seem to clearly work to counter that, instead focusing on much more nebulous attributes, I'm left with a very lacking understanding of what's actually wrong here. It's the equivalent of a movie-goer not understanding why the protagonist and antagonist are fighting, it harms the entire narrative arc because it's very difficult to engage with and attach to it.</p>
<p>The pliability of Hennessy that I pointed out last time didn't appear as much of an issue this time, so props for fixing that, but I still think the arc here is weakened by poor characterisation and weak setup and payoff. I think you need to put some thought into how Hennessy as a character is established and how the reader is made to care about him and his conflict - it's the root problem that's weakening the whole article, in my opinion. We're joining him right after all the context to this story, and the attempts to infer said context that has to be done makes the article feel poorly constructed and major elements feel ill-defined. Like I said before, there is a solid base here that could absolutely be made strong, but right now the character work and general execution leaves far too much in the air for it to work for me.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

