														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4819337">
									

<p>Hello,</p>
<p>I don't have a lot of comments about the grammar, etc, as this is pretty competently written. And given I am going to suggest other edits, it doesn't seem like a good idea to roil over SP&amp;G when it might get edited over, so, allow me to proceed.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Crit&nbsp;lives&nbsp;within</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Return&nbsp;to&nbsp;normlacy</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I have 3 central criticisms of this piece. One if procedural, two are content.</p>
<p><strong>Problem the First: Ben</strong><br />
Ben Hennessy is clearly anomalous, in addition to the 3 versions of him which appear to &quot;Help&quot; him grow and learn. Referring to these apparitions as SCP's but not the subject is&#8230;just flatly wrong to me. I think you've chosen to do this in an attempt to humanize the story a bit more, and I get that, but the lack of regard for Ben as an &quot;object&quot; seems very non-traditional or otherwise out of place to me. It's not&#8230;article breaking, but it's a formatting problem that is present from the very beginning and I have obviously latched onto it.</p>
<p>You may have to consider addressing Ben as SCP-XXXX-Prime or by some other designation. Calling him Hennessy in your logs is <em>probably</em> okay but you'd have to see how it looks.</p>
<p><strong>Problem the Second: Pacing</strong><br />
There is plenty of room on the wiki for longer character pieces like this, especially one that explores getting out of your own way and finding the resources to succeed within yourself. To that end, I have no problem with this article's length.</p>
<p>Having said that, several letters and then 4 somewhat lengthy log entries that follow it make for a lot of &quot;baseline&quot; followed by a slow walk up a gentle incline. If I were in your shoes, I would decide between the value of showing Hennessy's baseline versus showing the level of interference later on, and then trim whichever I valued less.</p>
<p>If it were me writing this, I would consider trimming down both. You wrote logs that each distinctly feature one of the clones. You could probably shorten *all* of these and/or achieve the same effect by involving multiple ones in the same scenes. Something to consider.</p>
<p>If you feel truly nothing can be trimmed to deliver the emotional payload you're looking for, that's your call as the author.</p>
<p><strong>Problem the Third: Stakes</strong><br />
The stakes in this article are extremely low and remain as such for the entirety of the piece. There is a simple exchange for any reader going on: You spend attention to read, and you need intrigue then payoff to keep going. &quot;What is going on here?&quot; or &quot;What will happen next?&quot; are very important questions and your piece really doesn't pose either of those.</p>
<p>From the onset, the problem is very clearly understood and we simply passively watch the main character resolve it. Then, we swoop in at the end with a previously unmentioned doctor and try to be the good guys and save the day.</p>
<p>Nothing through out this piece feels at risk. Ben gets frustrated and internalizes things. The Alter-Bens just disappear. Everyone stays nice and neat and tidy and in their place, and then Ben ends the article.</p>
<p>I see a resolution but I, as a reader, do not feel one.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

