<p>Hmmmm, the ways that the alter-Bens try to improve Ben ultimately are harmful to Ben's mental and at some points physical health. The other him do know this and therefore the worst-case scenario could involve Ben being completely disheartened by this and thinking that he can't improve at all and that these alternate versions of him are simply better than him/he doesn't feel like he can get to the points that all of them reached in their lives. Either way he gives up on trying to improve himself and falls back into a state of despair and spirals downwards thinking about his place in the world when so many other, better, versions of him exist.</p>
<p>Something like that could be the worst-case scenario, but I'm not quite sure how I would convey that idea in the story. Would he show doubt about his own abilities and keep comparing himself to them? Which they encourage more and more until he realises they aren't good for him? I don't know how I would go about that. But if any of that sounded like a good idea I could give it a shot.</p>

